@meta
id: 06-10
title: Backwards until, with T
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: find-t
par: 36
minutes: 4
vscode: End, then click between the space and the handler
open: webhooks.ts

@brief
The Stripe webhook is mounted without signature verification, which means
anyone who finds the URL can post refunds into the system. Add the missing
middleware and terminate the statement:

    app.use('/webhooks/stripe', rawBodyParser, verifySignature, stripeWebhookHandler);

You arrive at the right-hand end of the line to add the terminator. The
insert point is back to the left, in the gap that already exists after the
last comma — and it is that gap, rather than the comma itself, that you want
the cursor on.

@keys
| `F,` | backward onto the previous comma |
| `T,` | backward until just past the previous comma |
| `a` | insert to the right of the cursor |

@hints
Terminate the line first. That leaves you at the right-hand end, looking
leftwards.
--
`T` is the backwards `t`: it stops one character past the target, so `T,`
leaves you on the space that follows the comma rather than on the comma.
--
`A` `;` `Esc`, then `T,`, then `a`, and type `verifySignature, ` with a
trailing space, then `Esc`.

@solution
    A ; <Esc> T , a verifySignature,  <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Four keys together — `f`, `F`, `t`, `T` — and the whole system is two
questions. Which direction, and do you want to land on the character or
beside it. Lower case goes right, upper case goes left; `f` arrives on it,
`t` stops beside it.

`F,a` would have worked here too, with the space typed at the front of the
insert instead of the back. Neither is more correct. What matters is that
you decided where to land before you pressed anything, rather than landing
somewhere approximate and nudging with `h` and `l`. Approximate landings are
how one-character errors get into commits.

@start webhooks.ts
app.use('/webhooks/stripe', rawBodyParser, stripeWebhookHandler)

@expected webhooks.ts
app.use('/webhooks/stripe', rawBodyParser, verifySignature, stripeWebhookHandler);
